# ACE PL core rules

Before writing code, read the relevant parts of:
- `DECISIONS.md` (product rules — do not invent rules that aren't there; ask)
- `docs/PRD.md` (original product definition; `DECISIONS.md` overrides it)
- `docs/ENGINEERING_STANDARDS.md` (full standards)
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (layers, folders, data model)

## Non-negotiables
- Files ≤ 250 lines, components ≤ 200, functions ≤ 50, complexity ≤ 10, params ≤ 3.
  Exceed only if splitting is clearly worse; add `eslint-disable-next-line` with the reason.
- One purpose per file. Name files and functions by what they do (`calculate-standings.ts`, `calculateStandings`).
  Never `utils.ts`, `helpers.ts`, `misc.ts`, `data`, `handleStuff`.
- Nothing hardcoded: colours/fonts/spacing → `src/theme/`; rule values → `src/config/` or tournament settings; UI text → feature `copy.ts`.
- Business rules live in `features/<feature>/domain/` as pure functions with tests next to them.
- Routes in `src/app/` stay thin. Features import other features only via their `index.ts`.
- Schema changes only via `supabase/migrations/`. RLS on every table.

## Comments
- Explain why, never what. Short and plain, like a colleague.
- No banners, no emoji, no commented-out code, no "This function…" narration,
  no notes addressed to the reviewer ("Updated to fix…", "Added for…").

## UI copy
- Plain and direct, in the group's voice. Banter only where it fits (Fraud Watch, empty states).
- Never use: seamless, elevate, unlock, dive in, supercharge, effortless, journey, leverage,
  empower, revolutionize, game-changer, "Oops!". No emoji in UI chrome. No exclamation spam.

## Before finishing a task
- Type check, lint and tests pass.
- New domain logic has tests, including edge cases.
- If a product rule was unclear, ask instead of guessing; record agreed changes in `DECISIONS.md`.
